# gsre — Google SRE book notes

## SRE time allocation

```text
50% operations   — keep services stable and operable;
                   design them to be automatic and self-healing
50% development  — creative, autonomous engineering, informed by
                   the wisdom gleaned from running the service
```

In general, an SRE team is responsible for the availability, latency,
performance, efficiency, change management, monitoring, emergency response,
and capacity planning of their service(s).

Google operates under a blame-free postmortem culture, with the goal of
exposing faults and applying engineering to fix them, rather than avoiding
or minimizing them.

## Monitoring

Monitoring should never require a human to interpret any part of the
alerting domain. Software should do the interpreting; humans should be
notified only when they need to take action.

There are three kinds of valid monitoring output:

| Output | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Alerts | a human must act immediately — something is happening or about to happen |
| Tickets | a human must act, but not immediately; days of delay cause no damage |
| Logging | recorded for diagnostics/forensics; nobody reads it unless prompted |

## Homelab application

```sh
# The same idea at home: let systemd/journald do the watching,
# only alert when action is needed
systemctl --failed                                  # "alerts"
journalctl -p err -b --no-pager | tail -20          # "logging", read on demand
```
